    "payload": {
      "wordMax": 200,
      "wordMin": 180,
      "caseNotes": "SURGICAL WARD — REFERRAL ON DISCHARGE\nPatient: Mr Gregor Bauer\nAge: 58\nReferred to: Ms Priya Naidoo, Clinical Nurse Specialist, Outpatient Wound Care Clinic\nProcedure: 10 June 2026 · Discharged: 26 June 2026\n\nReason for referral:\nDehisced abdominal wound healing by secondary intention. Ongoing assessment and dressing management requested.\n\nOperation:\nEmergency laparotomy for perforated diverticulitis, with Hartmann's procedure. Lower midline incision.\nEnd colostomy in the left iliac fossa; stoma care is led by the stoma nursing team.\nAbdominal drain removed on day three without problems.\n\nPost-operative course:\nWound infection identified on day six with purulent discharge; swab grew mixed skin flora. Partial dehiscence of the lower third of the incision on day seven. Treated with intravenous antibiotics for five days, now on oral with five days remaining. Deep dehiscence excluded on examination by the surgical team.\nReviewed by the ward tissue viability nurse on 18 June, who agreed the current alginate dressing plan.\nExudate has reduced from heavy to moderate over the past week.\n\nCurrent wound status:\nCavity 4 cm by 2 cm, depth 1.5 cm. Moderate exudate. Granulating base, no slough and no necrotic tissue. Surrounding skin healthy with no erythema. Currently dressed with an alginate rope and a secondary absorbent dressing, changed on alternate days. Photograph taken 25 June and filed in the record.\nMeasurements on 20 June were 5 cm by 2.5 cm, depth 2 cm; the cavity is getting smaller.\nUpper two thirds of the incision intact and healed; sutures removed on 24 June.\nDressing last changed on the morning of 26 June, before discharge.\nWound pain 2/10 during dressing changes; he takes paracetamol about half an hour beforehand.\nA small supply of alginate rope and absorbent dressings has been given to take home.\n\nCurrent status:\nAfebrile for six days. Eating well. Mobile independently. Blood glucose 8 to 10 mmol/L; CRP 18, down from 180.\nTemperature 36.7 °C, pulse 78 regular and blood pressure 134/82 on the morning of discharge.\nCapillary glucose checked four times daily on the ward; no readings below 4 mmol/L.\nStoma active with formed output. Sleeping well.\nSeen by the dietitian on 22 June; eating three meals a day with snacks, no supplements needed.\nWeight 104 kg on admission and 101 kg at discharge.\n\nPast medical history:\nType 2 diabetes on metformin, HbA1c 62 in May 2026. Obesity, BMI 34. Hypertension. Ex-smoker, stopped 2020.\nAppendicectomy 1989.\nChildhood asthma, resolved; no inhalers needed since his teens.\nBowel cancer screening completed in 2024, normal at the time.\nInfluenza vaccine given at the practice last autumn.\n\nMedications:\nmetformin 1 g twice daily\nco-amoxiclav — five days remaining\namlodipine 5 mg daily\nparacetamol as required\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nPlan:\nTwice-weekly clinic dressing changes. Monitor for re-infection. Optimise glycaemic control to support healing — a diabetes review has been requested at the practice. Measure and record the wound weekly.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife. Works as an accountant and plans to return in three weeks.\nHis wife drives and has offered to bring him to every clinic appointment.\nOwns a home glucose meter and uses it confidently.\nKeen cyclist; hopes to rejoin his weekly club ride later in the summer.\nTheir daughter is getting married in September and he wants to be well for the day.\nChanges his own stoma bag confidently after teaching on the ward.\n\nAlso noted:\nAsked about lifting restrictions. Has a stoma nurse appointment on 30 June.\nAsked where to park at the outpatient building; given the hospital car park leaflet.\nLeft his phone charger on the ward; it was handed to his wife.\nRequested a copy of this letter for his own records.\n\nUnderstanding:\nMr Bauer understands that the wound will heal from the base upwards and that this may take several weeks.\nHe has watched two dressing changes and can describe the signs of infection: spreading redness, odour, more discharge or fever.\nHe knows to contact the ward or the clinic if these occur before his first appointment.\n\nWard housekeeping:\nDischarge medicines collected from the pharmacy at 11:00. Transport not needed; his wife collected him.\n\nPrevious healing:\nA sebaceous cyst removed from his back in 2019 healed without problems.\nNo previous wound breakdown after surgery.\n\nDischarge advice given:\nShown how to keep the dressing dry with a waterproof cover when showering.\nGiven written wound-care advice and the surgical ward telephone number.\nReminded to bring his medication list to the first clinic visit.\nDiscussed gentle daily walking; he plans to walk round the park near his home.",
      "recipient": "Ms Priya Naidoo, Clinical Nurse Specialist, Outpatient Wound Care Clinic",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a referral letter requesting ongoing assessment and dressing management of a dehisced abdominal wound."
    },
    "guidanceNote": "Describe the wound dimensions, current dressing regimen and the diabetes relevant to healing. The patient's occupation is minor context — the clinical detail the wound nurse needs is the wound status and glycaemic control."
