      "caseNotes": "EMERGENCY DEPARTMENT — TRANSFER TO CORONARY CARE\nPatient: Mr Raymond Bellini\nAge: 62\nTransferred to: Charge Nurse, Coronary Care Unit\nArrived: 26 June 2026, 14:20 · Transferred: 17:00 the same day\n\nReason for transfer:\nConfirmed non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction.\n\nPresentation:\nCentral crushing chest pain radiating to the left arm, present for two hours before arrival. Onset at rest. Associated sweating and nausea. Pain 8/10 on arrival.\nPain began while he was sitting in his taxi waiting for a fare.\nHe called 999 from the taxi rank and was brought in by ambulance.\nNo previous episodes of chest pain and no known history of angina.\nDescribed the pain as like a heavy weight pressing on his chest.\nHas had occasional indigestion after large meals for years, eased by antacids.\n\nFindings:\nECG at 14:30 — ST depression in the lateral leads, no ST elevation. Repeat at 16:00 unchanged. Troponin 480 ng/L on arrival, repeat at three hours rising to 910 ng/L. Chest X-ray no acute abnormality. Bloods otherwise unremarkable; potassium 4.1, creatinine 88.\nHaemoglobin 148 g/L; platelets within the normal range.\nCapillary glucose 6.2 mmol/L on arrival.\nMagnesium 0.9 mmol/L. Liver function tests normal.\n\nTreatment given in the department:\naspirin 300 mg, ticagrelor 180 mg, fondaparinux, glyceryl trinitrate infusion, morphine 5 mg with an antiemetic, oxygen titrated to saturation.\nPain fell from 8/10 to 3/10 within an hour of the infusion being started.\nAntiemetic given was ondansetron 4 mg intravenously at 14:50; nausea has settled.\nOxygen started at 4 litres by nasal cannula and reduced to 2 litres at 16:00.\n\nCurrent observations, 16:45:\nBlood pressure 138/82, pulse 76 regular, oxygen saturation 97% on 2 litres, respiratory rate 16, afebrile. Pain now settled, score 1/10. Cannula in the left forearm, patent, sited 14:35.\nEarlier observations, 15:30: blood pressure 146/88, pulse 84 regular, pain 3/10.\nSinus rhythm throughout; no arrhythmia seen on the monitor in the department.\nPassed urine at 16:10. Not catheterised.\n\nPast medical history:\nHypertension. Hyperlipidaemia. Current smoker, 20 a day. Appendicectomy aged 20.\nFractured right wrist in 2011 after a fall from a ladder, fully healed.\nBowel cancer screening completed last year, normal.\n\nFamily history:\nFather had a myocardial infarction in his fifties.\nMother alive, aged 88, with osteoporosis.\nYounger brother well, with no known heart disease.\n\nHome medications:\namlodipine 5 mg daily\natorvastatin 20 mg at night\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nPlan:\nContinue cardiac monitoring. Awaiting cardiology review for consideration of angiography. Repeat troponin and ECG in the morning. Bed rest. Observe for further chest pain or arrhythmia.\n\nSocial:\nMarried, works as a taxi driver. Visibly anxious about the diagnosis and has asked twice whether he will be able to work. His wife has been contacted and is on her way.\nHas said he would like help to stop smoking and accepted a leaflet about the hospital stop-smoking service.\nTheir son lives in Canada and has been told by text message.\nA colleague has collected his taxi from the hospital car park.\nSeason-ticket holder at his local football club; worried about missing Saturday's match.\nDrinks about 10 units of alcohol a week, mainly at weekends.\nLives in a first-floor flat with a lift.\n\nAlso noted:\nWears glasses for driving. Has not eaten since breakfast.\nAsked whether the television could be switched on in his cubicle.\nAsked where his wife can park; directed to the visitor car park.\nAsked to have his phone charged; a charger was lent by the department.\nWedding ring left on; no swelling of the fingers.\nLast dental check-up in the spring; no concerns.\nRisk score:\nGRACE score calculated as intermediate to high. Awaiting the cardiology decision on the timing of angiography.\nCardiology registrar aware of the patient and expected to review him on the unit this evening.\n\nLines and infusions in progress at transfer:\nGlyceryl trinitrate infusion running at 2 mg per hour, titrated to pain and blood pressure. Cardiac monitor in situ, leads intact.\n\nUnderstanding:\nTold by the emergency doctor that he has had a heart attack; he repeated this back accurately.\nUnderstands that a cardiologist will discuss whether he needs a test to look at the heart arteries.\n\nBelongings:\nPhone, wallet, car keys and glasses in a labelled bag travelling with him. Clothes to go home with his wife.\n\nDepartment housekeeping:\nCubicle 6 to be cleaned after transfer. Porter booked for 16:55.\n\nCoping:\nCalmer after the pain eased; chatting with staff and asking sensible questions about his tests.\nSays talking to his wife on the phone helped him settle.\nAsked whether he would be home in time for his granddaughter's birthday party next month.",
      "recipient": "Charge Nurse, Coronary Care Unit",
      "letterType": "transfer",
      "taskInstruction": "Write a transfer letter handing over a patient with a confirmed non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction."
