      "caseNotes": "ONCOLOGY WARD — TRANSFER SUMMARY\nPatient: Mrs Eleanor Castle\nAge: 71\nTransferred to: Sister Mary Quinn, Palliative Care Unit, St Anne's Hospice\nAdmitted: 18 June 2026 · Transferred: 26 June 2026\n\nReason for transfer:\nEnd-of-life care. Advanced pancreatic cancer with liver metastases. Active treatment ceased on 20 June following discussion with the oncology team.\nDiagnosed in February 2026 after weight loss and back pain.\nReceived palliative chemotherapy from March; stopped because of fatigue and disease progression.\n\nUnderstanding:\nThe patient and both daughters are aware of the diagnosis and the prognosis, which has been discussed as being short — likely days to a small number of weeks. The conversation took place on 20 June with the consultant, and Mrs Castle has spoken about it since without distress.\nShe has asked staff to tell her honestly how things are changing.\nPrefers to be called Ellie.\n\nCurrent status:\nBedbound since 22 June. Pain in the epigastrium and back, previously escalating, now controlled on a syringe driver. Nausea controlled. Poor oral intake — sips of fluid and occasional yoghurt. Mild confusion at night on two occasions, settling with reorientation and without medication. Pressure-area care two-hourly; sacral skin intact, Waterlow 18.\nPain scored 7/10 before the syringe driver was started on 21 June; scores since then 1 to 2 out of 10.\nNo vomiting since 21 June.\nSleeps for much of the day; usually wakes and chats with her daughters in the afternoon.\nLips and mouth moist with regular care; no oral thrush seen.\nBreathing comfortable; no noisy secretions heard.\nHeels and elbows intact; nursed on an air mattress.\nContinent of urine; wears a pad at night for reassurance.\n\nSymptom management:\nmorphine 30 mg over 24 hours via syringe driver\nmetoclopramide 30 mg over 24 hours via syringe driver\nmidazolam as required for agitation, used twice\nhyoscine butylbromide prescribed as required, not yet needed\nmouth care four-hourly\nSyringe driver line sited in the right upper arm on 24 June; site clean with no redness.\nSyringe refilled each morning at 10:00; the current syringe started at 10:00 on 26 June.\nDaughters have been shown how to give mouth care and like to help with it.\n\nPast medical history:\nType 2 diabetes, diet controlled — capillary glucose monitoring discontinued 23 June by agreement. Hypertension, antihypertensives stopped. Cholecystectomy 2009.\nBilateral cataract surgery 2018.\nMild osteoarthritis of the hands.\nMeasles in childhood.\nBreast screening had been up to date until last year.\nHysterectomy 1995 for fibroids.\n\nAllergies:\nPenicillin — rash.\n\nWishes and plans:\nDNACPR in place, signed 20 June, and the family is aware. She wishes to die peacefully and without further investigations. Catholic; requests chaplain visits, and the hospital chaplain has visited twice.\nReceived the sacrament of the sick from her parish priest on 21 June.\nRosary beads kept at her bedside, at her request.\n\nPlan:\nContinue comfort-focused care. Maintain the syringe driver and review as required. Honour the DNACPR. Involve the family fully. Spiritual support to continue.\n\nSocial:\nWidow. Two adult daughters, both involved and present daily. A grandson visits at weekends.\nElder daughter, Helen, is the first contact; younger daughter, Ruth, is the second.\nRetired primary school teacher; taught in the same village school for thirty years.\nThe grandson is in his second year studying engineering at university.\nA neighbour is looking after her cat.\nHelen lives ten minutes from the hospice; Ruth has two young children and usually visits in the mornings.\nHer husband died in 2017 after a long illness.\n\nAlso noted:\nEnjoys having the window open. Prefers not to have the television on.\nAsked whether the hospice has a garden she could be wheeled into in her bed.\nFlowers from her parish choir are on the ward and will go home with her daughters.\nLikes quiet classical music in the afternoons.\nAsked whether her own hairdresser could visit her at the hospice.\nEnjoys hearing the daily newspaper read aloud by her daughters.\n\nItems travelling with her:\nFamily photographs, her own blanket, glasses and a small bag of nightwear.\nCurrent drug chart and syringe driver record to accompany her in the ambulance.\n\nTransport:\nAmbulance booked for 14:00 on 26 June. Helen will follow by car.\n\nWard housekeeping:\nWard pressure-relieving mattress to stay on the ward; the hospice will provide its own.\n\nRecent days:\nOn 25 June she sat propped up for an hour and looked through family photographs with her grandson.\nShe told the ward sister she feels comfortable and settled.\nLast bed bath on the morning of 26 June; hair washed on 24 June.",
