      "caseNotes": "ACUTE STROKE UNIT — TRANSFER SUMMARY\nPatient: Mr Ivan Petrov\nAge: 68\nTransferred to: Nurse in Charge, Stroke Rehabilitation Ward\nAdmitted: 19 June 2026 · Transferred: 26 June 2026\n\nReason for transfer:\nRehabilitation following an acute ischaemic stroke.\n\nAdmission:\nSudden onset of left-sided weakness and slurred speech at 08:10 on 19 June, witnessed by his wife. CT confirmed a right middle cerebral artery ischaemic stroke. Thrombolysed at 09:40, within the window. Repeat imaging at 24 hours showed no haemorrhagic transformation.\nArrived by ambulance at 08:55 after his wife called 999.\nNo previous stroke or transient ischaemic attack.\nNIHSS 12 on arrival and 6 on 25 June.\n\nCurrent status:\nLeft arm power 2/5, left leg power 3/5. Mild facial droop, improving. Speech now intelligible with occasional slurring. Comprehension intact. Requires the assistance of two to transfer. Sits out of bed for two hours twice a day.\nOn admission left arm power was 1/5 and left leg 1/5; both have improved steadily.\nNo visual field loss on testing. No left-sided neglect identified by occupational therapy.\nSitting balance good; standing balance poor without support.\nMood low on 22 June; brighter since his wife began visiting daily.\nFeeds himself with his right hand using a plate guard.\nTires in the afternoons and rests on the bed after lunch.\nSpeaks slowly and is best understood face to face.\n\nSwallow:\nAssessed by speech and language therapy on 20 and 24 June. Currently on a soft diet with thickened fluids, level 2. Supervision required at meals. No coughing on the current consistency.\nEats about three quarters of each meal. Weight 82 kg on admission, 81 kg on 25 June.\nPrefers to eat sitting in his chair rather than in bed.\nMouth care after meals; no food left in the cheek seen.\n\nContinence:\nOccasional urinary incontinence, using continence aids. Bowels opened with lactulose.\nBowels last opened on 25 June, soft.\nBladder scan after voiding on 23 June showed 60 ml.\n\nSkin:\nAt risk. Pressure-area care two-hourly. Sacrum intact. Waterlow 16. Pressure-redistributing mattress in place.\nHeels checked each shift and intact.\nSmall graze on the left elbow from the day of admission, now healed.\n\nPast medical history:\nAtrial fibrillation, now anticoagulated — he had been on aspirin only before admission. Hypertension. Type 2 diabetes. Hernia repair 2015.\nAtrial fibrillation first diagnosed in 2023.\nTonsillectomy in childhood.\nRight knee arthroscopy 2010, no ongoing problems.\nInfluenza vaccine given last autumn.\nBlood pressure 130 to 145 systolic over the last three days.\nCapillary glucose 6 to 9 mmol/L on the ward. HbA1c 54 in April 2026.\nGout in the right big toe in 2019, no attacks since.\n\nMedications:\napixaban 5 mg twice daily\namlodipine 5 mg daily\nmetformin 500 mg twice daily\natorvastatin 80 mg at night\n\nAllergies:\nCodeine — intolerance (nausea), not an allergy.\n\nPlan:\nMultidisciplinary rehabilitation — physiotherapy, occupational therapy and ongoing speech and language therapy. Maintain swallow precautions until reassessed. Continue pressure care. Continence assessment. Mobility goals to be agreed with the patient.\n\nSocial:\nLives with his wife in a two-storey house with the bathroom upstairs. Retired carpenter. Highly motivated and states repeatedly that he wants to walk again.\nWife is in good health and keen to be involved in his rehabilitation.\nStairs have a banister on the left side going up.\nDaughter lives in Australia and has video calls with him most evenings.\nEnjoys woodwork and chess. They have a dog, which a neighbour is walking.\nBefore the stroke he was fully independent and walked the dog for an hour each day.\nHis wife does not drive and travels in by bus.\n\nAlso noted:\nWife visits daily and brings his own pyjamas. Wears a hearing aid on the right.\nHearing aid battery replaced on 24 June; spare batteries in his locker.\nAsked whether he can watch the snooker on the ward television.\nReading glasses in the bedside locker.\nPrefers to shave with his own electric razor.\nAsked for the ward Wi-Fi password.\nHis grandson sent a get-well card made at school, which is pinned above his bed.\n\nUnderstanding:\nMr Petrov understands that he has had a stroke and that recovery may take several months.\nHe and his wife have been given the stroke information pack.\n\nWard housekeeping:\nOwn clothes and toiletries packed. Porter booked for 11:00 on 26 June.\n\nTherapy so far:\nPhysiotherapy daily since 20 June, working on sitting balance and standing in a frame.\nOccupational therapy has started washing and dressing practice at the bedside.\nHe enjoys the sessions and asks for extra practice in the evenings.",
